.he "passwd (1)                   9/30/26                   passwd (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
passwd
- change a login password
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
passwd [ name ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Passwd changes the password of a login account.  With a name it
changes that account's password; with none it changes the password of
the user who is running it.
.sp
Only the super-user may change another account's password.  Anyone
may change their own.
.sp
If the account already has a password, the old one is asked for
first, and a wrong answer ends the command.  The super-user is not
asked for the old password, and there is nothing to ask for the first
time an account gets one, since an account starts with none and none
is demanded of it at log in.
.sp
The new password is then asked for twice, and the two answers must
agree.  Nothing typed for any of these questions is shown: the echo
is turned off while the line is read.
.sp
What the file keeps is not the password but a thirteen character hash
of it, so that the password cannot be read back out of the file - see
crypt (3).  Two accounts whose passwords are the same do not have the
same hash, because two characters of salt, taken from the clock, go
into it.  There is no way to recover a password that has been
forgotten; what can be done is to change it to one that is known.
.sp
A new password that is too short or too simple is refused and asked
for again.  The rule is the 1982 one.  At least four characters if
they mix letters of both cases with a digit or a punctuation mark; at
least five if they are letters of one case together with a digit; at
least six if they are letters of one case only; and a password of
digits alone is never accepted at any length.  Two refusals are
advice, and the third answer, whatever it is, is taken.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The password file is rewritten through a temporary, /etc/ptmp, a line
at a time, with the one line whose name matches replaced.  The
temporary is linked into place rather than copied over the old file.
There is no rename call on this machine, and link and unlink are what
one is built from here, so those two are what passwd uses.  If the
link cannot be made - a filesystem with no links, a full directory -
the copy is made the way the 1982 command made it, so that the
password file is never left missing.
.sp
A temporary left behind by an interrupted run has to be removed by
hand.  Passwd reports "Temporary file busy" and stops rather than
writing over it, which is also what the 1982 command did.
.sp
The questions are read by getpass and the hash is made by crypt, both
of them in libc - see getpass (3) and crypt (3).  Those are the same
two routines login (1) uses to ask for a password and to check one,
so a password set here is one that login will take.
.sp
v7 asked getlogin which account to change when no name was given.
There is no getlogin in this tree, so passwd reads the name that goes
with the uid it is running as out of the password file instead.  One
case follows from that: a uid with no entry of its own is reported as
an unknown user rather than as a usage error.
.sp
Passwd exits 0 when the password was changed and 1 when it was not,
which includes the case where the user changed their mind.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/passwd - the password file
.br
/etc/ptmp - the temporary it is rewritten through
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
login (1), su (1), crypt (3), getpass (3), getpwent (3), passwd (5)
